<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kerala is a state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where Malayalam is spoken. It was formed in 1956 from Travancore-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> and the Malabar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, and its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a>. For two thousand years its ports traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in 1957 it elected one of the world's first communist state governments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerala.jpg" alt="Kerala" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saad Faruque from Bangalore, India · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boathouse_(7063399547).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The weakened Pandyas were also attacked from <b>Kerala</b>, where the Venad ruler Ravivarman Kulasekhara advanced as far as Kanchipuram.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Govinda III also broke a southern coalition of Pallava, Pandya, <b>Kerala</b> and Ganga rulers and took tribute from Kanchipuram.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…land at the tip of the subcontinent had belonged to the Ay dynasty, the earliest rulers of southern <b>Kerala</b>, whose capital lay first at Aykudi and from the late 8th century at Kollam, where the Persian merchant Sulaiman al-Tajir found an extensive trade with China in the 9th…</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Syriac churches, among them the Syriac Orthodox Church, the Assyrian Church of the East, the Chaldean Catholic Church and the Maronite Church, still use it in their liturgies, and the Syriac Christians of <b>Kerala</b> in India preserve a heritage that goes back to Syriac missionaries.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It once reached further: Tamil or its dialects were the major language of administration, literature and daily life in <b>Kerala</b> until the 12th century CE, and Tamil inscriptions were made in southern Andhra Pradesh until then and in southern Karnataka from the 10th to the 14th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the southernmost part of the Indian subcontinent: present-day Tamil Nadu, <b>Kerala</b>, Puducherry, Lakshadweep and southern parts of Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
